terization and then they taste  like cannabis  so it really depends on the end product  you're going for  the fifth step is the third-party  testing although i would argue that  before you do that third-party testing  and in fact at each step through the  process  you should be doing in-house testing so  the in-house testing you'd want to be  doing  when you first receive the product  before milling because that's how you  ensure  that the grower is providing you with  a consistent starting material  that is to the specs so for example if  you  paid for a certain thc percentage you  want to ensure that the product that  is delivered with representative  sampling  is at that thc level and not just from  one little part of the bash  but throughout the whole batch and you  want to do it for every batch that's  delivered  because there's high variability from  growers and what they can produce  for you so once you know what you have  to start with  then you can do the extraction step  after the extraction step you want to do  another in-house  decarboxylation you can do real-time  testing to see  when the process is complete and after  winterization before sending out  by testing at each one of these steps  or after each one of these steps you're  able to optimize  using gmp your entire process so that  your end products  have reproducible cannabinoid  interpreting profiles  and you're able to optimize the amount  of time  taken for each batch step so you can run  as many batches as possible  with your existing set of instruments  now let's talk a little bit about the  forms of cannabis extracts  there's crumble batter butter shatter  distillate  crystalline dry sift rosin and bubble  hash  most of these forms the crumble batter  shatter crystalline dry sift rosin and  bubble hash  are end products that can be sold  directly to consumers  and are usually used in vape pens they  can be added for smoking  distillate is generally then used as an  ingredient  to make some other product it could be  something as simple as a vape cartridge  or it could be something more involved  like turning it into a tincture  or turning it into an edible so as we  were discussing before with the  decarboxylation step  decarboxylation is necessary for the  distillate for any application except  for the vape pen application  what are the methods of consuming  extracts well there's  edibles capsules tinctures vaping  dabbing and topicals  for all of these edible versions which  would be the edibles the capsules  the tinctures and the topicals none of  which  involve a heating process for those who  would do the decarboxylation  for vaping and dabbing it wouldn't be  necessary  so now i want to go on to the first  reason why a cannabis extraction lab  would want to set up an in-house testing  facility  so one of our customers who came to us  because they actually encountered a  problem  is a colorado-based cbd product  manufacturer  so they receive hemp they do a cbd  extraction  then they actually make a cbd topical  uh product they do use a pretty  basic workflow co2 extraction followed  by decarboxylation  then purification chirping enrichment  for the particular  terpenes that they're advertising for  which in this case is for pain relief  and then uh quality control which  previously they had been doing  only by sending out to a third party  testing lab  and that's when they encountered the  problem the problem  was test failure so they went through  this whole  expensive process starting with a fairly  large batch and they were getting a high  failure rate  we checked in with the state which  actually publishes this data and found  that in a given  time period 20 of the products the  extraction products  were failing in uh the third party  testing  so this represents a huge financial loss  for the extraction companies  which then either had to destroy the  product or put them back for  re-extraction which is very expensive  and leads to  lower yields so with failure rates this  high  you want to do everything you can to  minimize the failure rates which is  exactly what this cbd extractor did  if your final product is rejected  by the third-party testing lab at the  end  you have big costs associated with it  you have to either do a re-extraction or  throw away your product  you have a delay in the product delivery  so that if you had dispensaries  who were waiting for your product they  are going to be very unhappy customers  and you have the resulting loss of  customers and revenues  now this is if it fails even if your  product  passes the test you still have many  disadvantages  by only doing the uh third-party testing  the first one is time delays if you were  having  tests done for different aspects  of the extraction process say you want  the initial product to be tested to  ensure  that the potency that you're paying for  is the potency you're actually getting  that if you have if you have an organic  product and it should be pesticide free  that it actually is pesticide free if  you send that out to a third-party  testing lab  on average depending on your state and  the backlog  it can take several days so this will  slow down your whole process right you  have to actually  store all the materials while you ate  the test results  if you start the extraction then you may  end up needing to throw away those  materials if you find  that the uh product delivered to you was  not the product you had paid for  there's a higher cost for all of these  tests lab backlog  is basically another form of time delay  this is that  you are operating based upon the queue  that is in that third-party testing lab  and there are crunch times  so for example when oregon first did  recreationally  legalize cannabis during their rollout  the state  they had a very bad rollout but their  state the state only approved  two recreational testing labs in the  entire state  so naturally there was a tremendous  backlog  and the extractors were stuck because it  would take  months for them with only two labs in  the state to get the third party  approval  and then they had that 20 chance of a  rejection  so you're at the mercy of the third  party lab if you only rely on thirty  party labs for your testing and finally  the gmp intermediate step testing when  we're going over the extraction process  and we talked about the five steps  you want to do your own intermediate  test through each of those five steps  the uh gmp intermediate testing steps  and for this you need an in-house  testing lab  so benefit number one for setting up  your own  in-house testing lab is you save all of  this re-extraction costs  and you get your results faster now  let's go on to the second reason why you  would want  an in-house testing lab and that is  formulation  so we are now in a huge  increasingly sophisticated consumer  market  so i like to use the analogy of wine  if you walk into the supermarket you  won't just see  a bottle of wine for sale that's labeled  wine  it will say this is a red wine that is  done with this type of grape  from this particular region of the world  by this  winery it has these fruity undertones  and other things  you walk into a high-end cannabis  dispensary now  you will see very very similar product  offerings  cannabis has become a premium product or  at least at the upper end of the market  which you want to distinguish and the  way you distinguish it is with your  formulation  you say whatever it is you're looking  for if you are looking for  a product that is good for sleep we have  a formulation an  optimization of the cannabinoids and the  terpenes  that are good for sleep if you are  looking for something that is  stimulating and uplifting we have  something for that  on the medical side if you are looking  for anti-anxiety  there are specific formulations for that  now how does an extraction lab  create these formulations they do it  through  in-house testing so for your formulation  you want to set the dosage the dosage is  the dosage both of the thc which is  generally called potency  but it's also the whole cannabinoid  profile  some of the cannabinoids are better for  particular  medicinal effects than others you  also need to ensure your label accuracy  if you are advertising  we have created this particular  formulation for anti-anxiety  you want to ensure that your cannabinoid  interpreting profile supports that  a lot of cannabis companies are now  going organic  so they source organically grown uh  hemp or cannabis and then they create a  product  but you need to make sure that your  claim is validated by  testing the product received in your  extraction lab  for those pesticides and the easiest  fastest way to do that is with in-house  testing  and finally uh adverse patient reactions  heavy metals pesticides are all  mycotoxins there's all kinds  of problems that can be associated with  patient health and all of those will  bring liability  to the manufacturer if you are not  testing properly to ensure  that your product doesn't have any of  those  now i want to talk specifically about a  terpene  my favorite terpene linalool linalool is  a terpene  that's found naturally occurring in  lavender it's produced in rosemary and  it just  smells great if i smell a cannabis  product i can detect the linoleum  it has anti-anxiety properties it leads  to mental  alertness in animal models it's been  shown to be  highly effective as an antidepressant so  when i go to a dispensary  generally a good dispensary i will say  which of your products  are high in linalool and from a good  concentration manufacturer i can  actually see  the profile of terpenes and cannabinoids  and what their formulation is for that  particular product  so if you want to create a  brand of extract that targets specific  conditions and is at the high end of the  market you need to be very careful with  your formulation  which brings us to the second benefit by  having in-house testing  you are able to control the  complete cannabinoid profile of your  final product  you can optimize a cannabinoid profile  you can get consistent dosage and  formulation  you can control for the onset of action  because depending on  what is in the final product it has  different absorption rates  you get metabolic predictability and  improves safety and effectiveness  so we've now gone over two benefits to  having an in-house testing lab  the first one being so that you don't  fail  uh in your third party testing the  second being so that you can do  formulation and reformulation  in-house to create a wide range of  products each one of which  has a specific targeted formulation  the third reason gmp  good manufacturing practices all  cannabis extraction labs  should be following gmp it's done in the  pharmaceutical industry  it's done in the food production  industry and cannabis  is very similar whether you're growing  or extracting  do it the right way how do you follow  gmp  in the cannabis industry well it starts  with your sops  you need to have an sop for each step of  your manufacturing process  this includes the extraction side as  well as the testing side  you want your sops to be optimized and  you want all of your people to be  trained and to follow them  prompt and accurate documentation of the  work for compliance and traceability  every single sample that you run needs  to be traceable back to a qc  sample a spike a duplicate a blank  whatever it is that's part of 17.25  compliance on the third party and local  testing side  it's part of gmp compliance on the  extraction side  you need to maintain your facility and  all your equipment this means  calibration and maintenance as for the  schedule  and it gets even more complicated as we  go in later because a lot of the  equipment  complicated instruments such as lcms  will have multiple sub-components each  one of which  has their own schedule for calibration  and maintenance  and finally define develop and prove the  job competency this is the training of  your staff  all of your staff need to be trained for  each thing they're going to be doing in  the lab both on the testing side  and the extraction side so this brings  us  to our third and final benefit for  setting up your own in-house testing lab  which is you can get higher yields  because using trend analysis you can  optimize the amount of time you spend  in the extraction process in the  decarboxylation process and in the  winterization process  you get better product consistency by  controlling at  each step that you're getting the same  potency the same cannabinoid profile  your final end product will also be  consistent and reduce liability since  you're able to test  right from the beginning for heavy  metals pesticides mycotoxins etc  so benefits of an in-house testing lab  the benefits  are at each step of your process in your  extraction  step right from the beginning before you  start the extraction you're able to test  the true potency of the flower which has  been given to you by the grower  you're able to look at your extraction  efficiency  which is average efficiencies can be as  low as  30 percent so anything you can do to  increase that efficiency  will make your product far more cost  competitive  so you're able to determine your  extraction efficiency and keep working  on improving it  you can get the potency of your crude  oil right after it comes out of the  extraction before the next step  so you can see at which step in your  process are you losing your thc are you  losing your product  so that you can keep optimizing for that  post-processing the main post-processing  step is the decarboxylation  you want to determine in real time  because it's quite variable  when to stop the decarboxylation process  to do that  you need to measure how much of the  product is decarboxylated in real time  and that can only be done with an  in-house testing lab you can't there's  no time to send it out to a third-party  lab  and finally the formulation this is not  the formulation that we were discussing  before which is creating new products  this is once you have a formula with  every batch  you want that batch to consistently  follow your formulation  if there is a variability in the product  given to you to start with  they have a slightly different  cannabinoid profile slightly different  potency  you can adjust for that in the  extraction process  so that your end product meets your  formulation  even though there was variability with  your starting product or  something may have gone wrong in one of  the steps of your extraction process  to do that you need an in-house testing  lab that will tell you as you do it  oh look after this step we had a lower  potency than we expected  so now in the following step we're gonna  have to use higher concentrations  in order to bring the potency back up so  it helps you with your final formulation  keeping it consistent so i hope by now  i've convinced you that in your cannabis  extraction lab  it's very important to have an in-house  testing lab because that's the only way  you're going to be able to  avoid third-party testing do your  formulation  and follow gmp so now let's talk about  limbs and how a limbs will help you with  all of that  the limbs can help you in a whole bunch  of ways it can ensure the quality of the  cannabis sample  so basically there is a whole sop for  how you should be preparing  the sample or any type of test you're  going to do for it  those sops are all contained within the  limbs it does the tracking of the  maintenance and calibration of the  testing equipment  as well as the extraction equipment  it helps you with the documentation the  record keeping  for all the laboratory personnel and  finally  we went through the list of the gmp  requirements it helps you follow all of  the gmp requirements  equipment instrumentation instrument  calibration and  