ds so with failure rates this  high  you want to do everything you can to  minimize the failure rates which is  exactly what this cbd extractor did  if your final product is rejected  by the third-party testing lab at the  end  you have big costs associated with it  you have to either do a re-extraction or  throw away your product  you have a delay in the product delivery  so that if you had dispensaries  who were waiting for your product they  are going to be very unhappy customers  and you have the resulting loss of  customers and revenues  now this is if it fails even if your  product  passes the test you still have many  disadvantages  by only doing the uh third-party testing  the first one is time delays if you were  having  tests done for different aspects  of the extraction process say you want  the initial product to be tested to  ensure  that the potency that you're paying for  is the potency you're actually getting  that if you have if you have an organic  product and it should be pesticide free  that it actually is pesticide free if  you send that out to a third-party  testing lab  on average depending on your state and  the backlog  it can take several days so this will  slow down your whole process right you  have to actually  store all the materials while you ate  the test results  if you start the extraction then you may  end up needing to throw away those  materials if you find  that the uh product delivered to you was  not the product you had paid for  there's a higher cost for all of these  tests lab backlog  is basically another form of time delay  this is that  you are operating based upon the queue  that is in that third-party testing lab  and there are crunch times  so for example when oregon first did  recreationally  legalize cannabis during their rollout  the state  they had a very bad rollout but their  state the state only approved  two recreational testing labs in the  entire state  so naturally there was a tremendous  backlog  and the extractors were stuck because it  would take  months for them with only two labs in  the state to get the third party  approval  and then they had that 20 chance of a  rejection  so you're at the mercy of the third  party lab if you only rely on thirty  party labs for your testing and finally  the gmp intermediate step testing when  we're going over the extraction process  and we talked about the five steps  you want to do your own intermediate  test through each of those five steps  the uh gmp intermediate testing steps  and for this you need an in-house  testing lab  so benefit number one for setting up  your own  in-house testing lab is you save all of  this re-extraction costs  and you get your results faster now  let's go on to the second reason why you  would want  an in-house testing lab and that is  formulation  so we are now in a huge  increasingly sophisticated consumer  market  so i like to use the analogy of wine  if you walk into the supermarket you  won't just see  a bottle of wine for sale that's labeled  wine  it will say this is a red wine that is  done with this type of grape  from this particular region of the world  by this  winery it has these fruity undertones  and other things  you walk into a high-end cannabis  dispensary now  you will see very very similar product  offerings  cannabis has become a premium product or  at least at the upper end of the market  which you want to distinguish and the  way you distinguish it is with your  formulation  you say whatever it is you're looking  for if you are looking for  a product that is good for sleep we have  a formulation an  optimization of the cannabinoids and the  terpenes  that are good for sleep if you are  looking for something that is  stimulating and uplifting we have  something for that  on the medical side if you are looking  for anti-anxiety  there are specific formulations for that  now how does an extraction lab  create these formulations they do it  through  in-house testing so for your formulation  you want to set the dosage the dosage is  the dosage both of the thc which is  generally called potency  but it's also the whole cannabinoid  profile  some of the cannabinoids are better for  particular  medicinal effects than others you  also need to ensure your label accuracy  if you are advertising  we have created this particular  formulation for anti-anxiety  you want to ensure that your cannabinoid  interpreting profile supports that  a lot of cannabis companies are now  going organic  so they source organically grown uh  hemp or cannabis and then they create a  product  but you need to make sure that your  claim is validated by  testing the product received in your  extraction lab  for those pesticides and the easiest  fastest way to do that is with in-house  testing  and finally uh adverse patient reactions  heavy metals pesticides are all  mycotoxins there's all kinds  of problems that can be associated with  patient health and all of those will  bring liability  to the manufacturer if you are not  testing properly to ensure  that your product doesn't have any of  those  now i want to talk specifically about a  terpene  my favorite terpene linalool linalool is  a terpene  that's found naturally occurring in  lavender it's produced in rosemary and  it just  smells great if i smell a cannabis  product i can detect the linoleum  it has anti-anxiety properties it leads  to mental  alertness in animal models it's been  shown to be  highly effective as an antidepressant so  when i go to a dispensary  generally a good dispensary i will say  which of your products  are high in linalool and from a good  concentration manufacturer i can  actually see  the profile of terpenes and cannabinoids  and what their formulation is for that  particular product  so if you want to create a  brand of extract that targets specific  conditions and is at the high end of the  market you need to be very careful with  your formulation  which brings us to the second benefit by  having in-house testing  you are able to control the  complete cannabinoid profile of your  final product  you can optimize a cannabinoid profile  you can get consistent dosage and  formulation  you can control for the onset of action  because depending on  what is in the final product it has  different absorption rates  you get metabolic predictability and  improves safety and effectiveness  so we've now gone over two benefits to  having an in-house testing lab  the first one being so that you don't  fail  uh in your third party testing the  second being so that you can do  formulation and reformulation  in-house to create a wide range of  products each one of which  has a specific targeted formulation  the third reason gmp  good manufacturing practices all  cannabis extraction labs  should be following gmp it's done in the  pharmaceutical industry  it's done in the food production  industry and cannabis  is very similar whether you're growing  or extracting  do it the right way how do you follow  gmp  in the cannabis industry well it starts  with your sops  you need to have an sop for each step of  your manufacturing process  this includes the extraction side as  well as the testing side  you want your sops to be optimized and  you want all of your people to be  trained and to follow them  prompt and accurate documentation of the  work for compliance and traceability  every single sample that you run needs  to be traceable back to a qc  sample a spike a duplicate a blank  whatever it is that's part of 17.25  compliance on the third party and local  testing side  it's part of gmp compliance on the  extraction side  you need to maintain your facility and  all your equipment this means  calibration and maintenance as for the  schedule  and it gets even more complicated as we  go in later because a lot of the  equipment  complicated instruments such as lcms  will have multiple sub-components each  one of which  has their own schedule for calibration  and maintenance  and finally define develop and prove the  job competency this is the training of  your staff  all of your staff need to be trained for  each thing they're going to be doing in  the lab both on the testing side  and the extraction side so this brings  us  to our third and final benefit for  setting up your own in-house testing lab  which is you can get higher yields  because using trend analysis you can  optimize the amount of time you spend  in the extraction process in the  decarboxylation process and in the  winterization process  you get better product consistency by  controlling at  each step that you're getting the same  potency the same cannabinoid profile  your final end product will also be  consistent and reduce liability since  you're able to test  right from the beginning for heavy  metals pesticides mycotoxins etc  so benefits of an in-house testing lab  the benefits  are at each step of your process in your  extraction  step right from the beginning before you  start the extraction you're able to test  the true potency of the flower which has  been given to you by the grower  you're able to look at your extraction  efficiency  which is average efficiencies can be as  low as  30 percent so anything you can do to  increase that efficiency  will make your product far more cost  competitive  so you're able to determine your  extraction efficiency and keep working  on improving it  you can get the potency of your crude  oil right after it comes out of the  extraction before the next step  so you can see at which step in your  process are you losing your thc are you  losing your product  so that you can keep optimizing for that  post-processing the main post-processing  step is the decarboxylation  you want to determine in real time  because it's quite variable  when to stop the decarboxylation process  to do that  you need to measure how much of the  product is decarboxylated in real time  and that can only be done with an  in-house testing lab you can't there's  no time to send it out to a third-party  lab  and finally the formulation this is not  the formulation that we were discussing  before which is creating new products  this is once you have a formula with  every batch  you want that batch to consistently  follow your formulation  if there is a variability in the product  given to you to start with  they have a slightly different  cannabinoid profile slightly different  potency  you can adjust for that in the  extraction process  so that your end product meets your  formulation  even though there was variability with  your starting product or  something may have gone wrong in one of  the steps of your extraction process  to do that you need an in-house testing  lab that will tell you as you do it  oh look after this step we had a lower  potency than we expected  so now in the following step we're gonna  have to use higher concentrations  in order to bring the potency back up so  it helps you with your final formulation  keeping it consistent so i hope by now  i've convinced you that in your cannabis  extraction lab  it's very important to have an in-house  testing lab because that's the only way  you're going to be able to  avoid third-party testing do your  formulation  and follow gmp so now let's talk about  limbs and how a limbs will help you with  all of that  the limbs can help you in a whole bunch  of ways it can ensure the quality of the  cannabis sample  so basically there is a whole sop for  how you should be preparing  the sample or any type of test you're  going to do for it  those sops are all contained within the  limbs it does the tracking of the  maintenance and calibration of the  testing equipment  as well as the extraction equipment  it helps you with the documentation the  record keeping  for all the laboratory personnel and  finally  we went through the list of the gmp  requirements it helps you follow all of  the gmp requirements  equipment instrumentation instrument  calibration and  maintenance you've got a lot of  instruments you have instruments for  calibration  you have instruments for your testing  both sets of equipment  need to be calibrated and maintained  here we can see an agilent hplc  this agilent hplc has a calibration date  which is in december a next calibration  date  in september and five days before  the next calibration date the looms will  automatically  send a reminder an email reminder as  well as  in-app letting the lab manager know that  the instrument needs to be calibrated  in addition to that and this is the  added layer of comp of complexity  this instrument has three sub components  which you can see listed on the right  every one of those sub components has  its own calibration and maintenance  schedule  so if you were doing this manually you  would have  a booklet of instrument calibration  with no alerts so it's much better to do  this in the limbs so that you're  actually getting alerts  and you know ahead of time when an  instrument needs to be calibrated and  maintained so you have plenty of time to  call a technician in  testing calibration methods so you now  have your instruments tested and  calibrated  but in order to recalibrate them when  the neck  when the alarm goes off you need to have  an sop for how your test and calibration  method should be done  these are generally provided to you by  the instrument manufacturer  the challenge is because this is a new  industry  the instrument manufacturers keep  updating  the test calibration and sops associated  with any instrument  when they issue you a new one you need  to ensure  that every technician gets retrained on  it  and only the latest calibration and  testing  sop comes up so the limbs will do all  that for you  you can see here that uh we had a  particular version and then we replaced  that version with the latest version  the lims will only have the latest  version comes up so that when the tech  transition comes in  to do the calibration the technician is  following the latest calibration method  associated instruments testing the  quality of results  this is about the traceability so this  is absolutely essential for 17.025  compliance in a third-party testing lab  but it is useful in an in-house testing  lab  what it means is that if there is  liability  if there is a question well how do we  know  that the instrument that the test result  that you gave was done on an  instrument that was properly maintained  and calibrated  not today but at the time the test was  done  so you need an association between the  calibration  date and the date on which the test was  done  the limbs will track all of that the  limbs will say here you can see with our  traceability  that the particular test was done on  such and such date  here was the previous calibration here  was the sop that was followed for that  calibration  so you have a completely traceable  record showing that your instrument was  calibrated and maintained  manage documents with limbs this is all  of your sops  so the second thing that would come up  in that same liability question  is when the test was done was the  correct sop followed  this will show it to you only the latest  sop was available  and the sop that was used for any  particular test distract  this is useful in the case of a recall  of a test method  the instrument manufacturers this  doesn't happen often but they can say  there was something wrong with the sop  we issued  and the test results need to be  invalidated instead of needing to  invalidate  all of your tests with the limbs it'll  tell you  only these tests were done using this  sop  so these are the only tests we need to  redo  manage staff and training you have a  whole bunch of staff and they all need  to be trained  for each step of your extraction process  and  each step of your testing process so  here you can see two different training  courses  ion exchange chromatography and paper  chromatography  and in the ion exchange chromatography  we had five students  some did well some not so well so these  are made up names but with our made up  list of names  maxim gilbert uh he did well sonya  fernandez she did well  christina paul not so well tim yan not  so well  they were not found to be competent so  the results of this  training course are then reflected in  the drop down  when you go to assign staff to conduct a  test  in the drop down only the names of those  staff who've been found to be competent  for that particular test would appear  so that way as part of 17025 and part of  gmp  you can say well my extraction is only  being done  by laboratory technicians who are found  to be competent  and properly trained for that particular  process  quality control samples so we touched on  briefly before  anytime you do a test you need to have  traceability  back to a qc sample a blank a duplicate  so you need to be able to show i ran 10  samples then i ran a qc  sample so we know that the result is  accurate because the result for that qc  sample was accurate  and finally trend analysis this is where  i think that a lens has  the maximum benefit for an extraction  lab  you are able to keep all of your data  for all of the tests and this is  especially useful  if you've been doing those uh in process  steps so that you know the initial  product you know  what was your potency after doing the  initial extraction  you know the decarboxylation time and  you know what was your potency after  your winterization step  so with trend analysis you were able to  track the variability  at each stage from batch to batch  and hopefully over time you will make  the variability from batch keep  decreasing  so it is less and less and less by doing  this you can optimize for two things  you can optimize for your yield so that  let's say you're running an extraction  you can run an extraction forever and  you'll keep getting  more and more of the product out but  it's a diminishing return  so at some point it is it is  if greater benefit for you to stop the  process  to throw away that starting material and  put in a new batch and start your second  batch  you'll only know what that time is by  doing trend analysis  with trend analysis you get to optimize  the timing for your entire extraction  process  so this is where a limbs and an in-house  testing lab  are extremely important which is for the  optimization of your entire process  with trend analysis you can optimize the  time for decarboxylation  for winterization and for extraction  trend analysis you get the optimization  extraction process you can visualize  your gmp  you can ensure that you're getting a  consistent cbd and terpene profile  and it provides you with a detailed  overview of the performance  of your laboratory enabling you to  compare your yields to other extraction  labs  so that the lab manager can then take  corrective actions  so using a limbs gives you more accurate  results  greater productivity a higher quality  and more consistent product  and lower cost since you've been able to  optimize your yields  as well as throughput and finally you  have a happy extraction lab  you have a reduction in compliance  failures you have higher throughput and  higher yields  you have a wide product range since  you're able to create new formulations  using your in-house testing lab  and you have a reputation for  consistency of the final product among  customers  so i hope that this has been useful to  you and that you've  learned the reasons why extraction labs  in my opinion  should all have in-house testing and how  a limbs can help you  thank you very much
 

